output.summary:
q04 got 8.7%, 13.0% and 16.5% faster across three bench runs (about 237→216, 252→219 and 239→199 ms). Everything else was within noise: q09 was +8.4% in the first full run but +2.4% on rerun, and q16 went from +5.3% to +0.2%. The full-bench geomean was -0.6%. The tree builds, and the change is ready to judge.

What changed:
(1) deliminator.cpp: a new static helper, SelectionIsReplaceableByJoinFilter, lets Optimize remove the DelimGet join even when the outer side has a filter. It applies only when all of these hold:
- the delim join is SEMI;
- there is exactly one DelimGet and one join with it;
- that join is INNER or SEMI, with only `=` conditions between plain column refs;
- the subquery side contains only projections, filters, table scans, DelimGets and INNER/SEMI comparison joins.
In that case the delim join becomes a plain SEMI join.
(2) Extra step not in the plan: the remaining join kept `IS NOT DISTINCT FROM`, and join filter pushdown skips that comparison, so nothing was pushed at first. Deliminating already adds `IS NOT NULL` on the column that replaces the DelimGet column. So on this path I switch a condition to `=` when its right side is a column ref that is, or passes through projections to, one of those non-null columns. For a SEMI join, `=` and `IS NOT DISTINCT FROM` give the same rows when the right side can't be NULL.
(3) join_filter_pushdown_optimizer.cpp: removed RIGHT_SEMI and RIGHT_ANTI from the GenerateJoinFilters bail-out. Both output only build rows, so dropping probe rows that cannot match is safe. I found no other join-type gate in the physical hash join's filter pushdown code.

q04 plan now: a plain RIGHT_SEMI hash join on `o_orderkey = o_orderkey`, and min/max dynamic filters reach the lineitem scan. The Bloom filter does not: compressed materialization compresses the join key, and FinalizeFilters skips the Bloom filter whenever the pushed column has a storage offset or a type cast. So about 37.9M lineitem rows still reach the join instead of the planned ~2.3M. The gain comes from removing the DISTINCT agg and the second join.

Correctness checks: hand-built VALUES tables with NULLs on both sides for EXISTS, NOT EXISTS, IN and a multi-join subquery gave correct results. On the benchmark data I ran six queries:
- q04-style EXISTS and NOT EXISTS;
- a q21-style EXISTS with `<>` (keeps the old path);
- customer/orders EXISTS;
- EXISTS over a two-table join in the subquery;
- EXISTS whose correlation key is NULL on some rows.
All six counts matched `SET disabled_optimizers='join_filter_pushdown,deliminator'`. bench's result check passed on all visible templates. I did not run the sqllogictests the issue suggested; I only have access to bench, profile and sql.

